import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "The database",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform's database: Postgres, or SQLite or Turso inside the server's process, as the app's program says. Queries whose rows decode into the record you annotate, transactions that take jobs with them, migrations built into the executable, notifications, SQL checked by the database's own engine (rocx check --sql), and roc-pg, a client in Roc whose queries are checked when the app is built.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "choose", label: "Postgres or SQLite" },
		{ id: "query", label: "A query" },
		{ id: "rows", label: "Rows, as records" },
		{ id: "write", label: "Writing" },
		{ id: "transactions", label: "Transactions" },
		{ id: "migrations", label: "Migrations" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where it can be used" },
		{ id: "notify", label: "Notifications" },
		{ id: "checked", label: "Checking queries: rocx check --sql" },
		{ id: "turso", label: "Turso" },
		{ id: "roc-pg", label: "roc-pg: queries checked at build" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · the database"
				title="One database, and the types you say"
			>
				An app has one database: Postgres, or SQLite running inside the server's own process. The
				<code>Db</code> module runs a statement with its parameters and gives back the rows, decoded into
				the record type you annotate. Transactions keep one connection for a call, and migrations run once
				each as the server starts. The actors' state and the jobs queue live in the same database.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="choose">Postgres or SQLite</Heading>
			<p>
				An app picks its database in its program and writes its SQL for that database: nothing is
				translated between the two.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Postgres</code></strong> is the default. The server finds it at
					<code>--database-url</code> (or <code>DATABASE_URL</code>); without one, the app has no database.
					Choose it for data other programs share, or more data than one server's disk holds.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Sqlite(path)</code></strong> is a file inside the server's own process, at
					<code>path</code> relative to where the server starts. The file (and its directory) is made when
					missing, so the app runs with nothing to set up and nothing beside it. It suits an app on one
					server, which is what the platform runs: every example app with a database uses it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>Turso(path)</code></strong> is SQLite's SQL on another engine, which lets
					transactions write at the same time (<a href="#turso">below</a>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started| Ok({}),\n\tmiddleware: Middlewares.default,\n\tdatabase: Sqlite(\"data/plate.db\"),\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>--database-url sqlite:/var/lib/plate.db</code> puts a SQLite app's file somewhere else (on a
				volume, or a test's own file). A URL for the other kind of database stops the server as it starts,
				saying which one the app wants.
			</p>
			<p>
				A package says which databases it works with, in its <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a>'s
				needs: <code>Database</code> (either), <code>Postgres</code> or <code>Sqlite</code>. An app whose
				database doesn't match stops as it starts, saying so. A package with SQL for both asks
				<code>Db.backend!</code> which to run, and lists a migration for each
				(<code>{"{ name: \"0001_jobs\", sql: lite, on: Sqlite }"}</code>), as the jobs plugin does.
			</p>

			<Heading id="query">A query</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.all!</code> runs a statement with its parameters and returns its rows. The annotation says
				what a row is. From the platform's test app:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_notes.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_notes.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Parameters are written <code>$1</code>, <code>$2</code>… on both databases, and each is a
				<code>Db.Value</code>: <code>Null</code>, <code>Text</code>, <code>Int</code> or <code>Bool</code>.
				Postgres receives each as text and reads it as the type its place wants (<code>$1</code> in
				<code>where id = $1</code> becomes a <code>bigint</code>). SQLite receives each as it is (a
				<code>Bool</code> as 1 or 0), and a number column accepts text that is a number. Never build a
				statement from strings: pass values as parameters.
			</p>
			<p>
				On Postgres, <code>Db.array</code> passes a list as one parameter: an array, written as Postgres
				writes one in text (<code>{"{\"work\",\"to do\",NULL}"}</code>), which Postgres reads as the array
				its place wants, of text or of numbers: <code>= any($1)</code>, <code>{"@> $1"}</code>,
				<code>$1::bigint[]</code>, <code>unnest($1::text[])</code>. (Alone, <code>unnest($1)</code> can't
				tell which array, so it needs the cast.) Each text is quoted, so a comma, a brace or the word NULL
				inside one stays text. SQLite has no arrays: pass a list as JSON text and read it with
				<code>json_each</code> (<code>where tag in (select value from json_each($1))</code>).
			</p>
			<Code
				title="An array as a parameter"
				source={"tagged : Try(List({ id : I64 }), [DbErr(Str), BadRow(Str)])\ntagged = Db.all!(\"select id from notes where tags @> $1\", [Db.array(tags.map(|tag| Text(tag)))])\n\nDb.run!(\"delete from notes where id = any($1)\", [Db.array(ids.map(|id| Int(id)))])"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>

			<Heading id="rows">Rows, as records</Heading>
			<p>
				The host reads each row's columns, as the database typed them, and hands Roc the rows as JSON.
				<code>Db.all!</code> decodes each row into the record you annotated, matching fields to columns by
				name. A row that doesn't fit is <code>BadRow</code>, saying why.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Nulls are typed.</strong> Read a column that may be null as <code>Db.Nullable(a)</code>:
					<code>Null</code> or <code>Some(a)</code>, matched like any tag union. The record decides what may
					be null. A null in a column the record reads as a plain value is <code>BadRow</code>, naming the
					column.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>From Postgres:</strong> booleans, integers, floats, text, <code>json</code> and
					<code>jsonb</code>, <code>uuid</code>, and timestamps and dates (as text). Any other type needs a
					cast (<code>::text</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>From SQLite:</strong> each value as it is (an integer or a real as a number, text as a
					string), a column declared <code>boolean</code> as a <code>Bool</code>, and one declared
					<code>json</code> as its JSON. A boolean computed in a statement (<code>exists (…)</code>) is 0 or
					1, an <code>I64</code>. SQLite has no time type: store milliseconds in an integer
					(<code>Time.now_millis!</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="write">Writing</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.run!</code> runs a statement and returns how many rows it changed. <code>Db.script!</code>
				runs several statements with no parameters, one after another (separated by <code>;</code>), as one
				transaction: if one fails, none stays.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_insert.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_insert.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="transactions">Transactions</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.transaction!</code> runs a function on one connection. What it writes, and the jobs it
				queues, stay only if it returns <code>Ok</code>. An <code>Err</code>, a failing statement or a crash
				rolls all of it back, and a call that ends with a transaction still open has it rolled back.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_transaction.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_transaction.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Inside, you can use <code>?</code> on what <code>Db</code>, <code>Job</code> and your own code return,
				with no error mapping. Their errors are open tag unions (<code>Db.run!</code>'s is
				<code>[DbErr(Str), ..others]</code>, <code>Job.enqueue!</code>'s <code>[JobErr(Str), ..others]</code>),
				so the transaction's error is all of them together. With an app's own <code>Err(Refused)</code>, it
				is <code>[DbErr(Str), JobErr(Str), Refused]</code>, and the caller matches each case.
			</p>
			<p>
				A job queued in a transaction is a row in it, so it runs only if the transaction commits, and the
				workers are woken when it does (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a>).
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Keep transactions short on SQLite">
				<p>
					On SQLite, reads go on beside a write, but writes take turns: a transaction holds the write lock
					from its start, so every other write waits until it ends. Keep anything slow (a request to
					another server, a wait) out of a transaction. On Postgres it would hold only the rows it wrote.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="migrations">Migrations</Heading>
			<p>
				An app's schema is a list of migrations, built into its executable. In an app that
				<code>rocx build</code> builds, put each one in a <code>.sql</code> file in the app's
				<code>migrations/</code> directory (<code>0001_tables.sql</code>, <code>0002_seed.sql</code>…):
				the build embeds them, in the order of their names. A hand-written program lists them in
				<code>program.migrations</code>, each a name and its SQL, imported from a file:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_migrations.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_migrations.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.platform_migration.file} source={Sources.platform_migration.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain={Bool.True} />
			<p>
				When the server starts, it applies each migration it hasn't applied yet, in order, each in a
				transaction of its own, and records it by name in <code>rocx_migrations</code>. The platform's own
				tables (<code>rocx_actors</code>, <code>rocx_actor_timers</code>) come first, then the plugins'
				(the jobs plugin's <code>rocx_jobs</code>, recorded as <code>jobs/0001_jobs</code>), then the app's.
				A migration that fails stops the server, saying why. If two servers start at once (a deploy's old
				one and its new one), they take turns: on Postgres through an advisory lock, on SQLite through its
				write lock.
			</p>

			<Heading id="where">Where it can be used</Heading>
			<p>
				Anywhere only the server runs: an endpoint, a middleware, a job, an actor, a stream. A component is
				compiled for the browser too, so it can't query the database itself. It reads its data through a
				GET endpoint (<code>Endpoint.get!</code>), which the server answers in-process while it renders the
				page. The handler runs only on the server, so it may query; the browser's copy has only the
				endpoint's method and URL (<a href={"${Paths.calls}#user"}>who is asking</a>).
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Connections.</strong> A statement takes a connection from a pool (16 for Postgres, 8 for
					SQLite's or Turso's file) and gives it back; a transaction keeps one until it ends. Statements are
					prepared once per connection, and kept. SQLite's and Turso's calls run on threads of their own,
					so a call waiting on one holds none of Roc's.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Postgres</strong> connections use TLS when the database offers it (or the URL's
					<code>sslmode</code> asks for it), checked against the web's certificate authorities. In this
					repository, <code>just db</code> runs a Postgres in Docker and prints its URL.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No database?</strong> A Postgres app started without a URL still runs; each query answers
					that there is no database, and <code>Db.connected!</code> says so up front.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>SQLite's file</strong> is in WAL mode, with its <code>-wal</code> and <code>-shm</code>
					files beside it. To back it up, run <code>sqlite3 data/plate.db ".backup copy.db"</code>, or copy
					the file while the server is stopped (Turso's only that way: the server holds it while it runs).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="notify">Notifications</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Db.notify!(channel, payload)</code> tells whoever listens to <code>channel</code>: when the
				call's transaction commits (never if it rolls back), or at once outside a transaction. A call
				listens with <code>Db.listen!(channel)</code>, then hears each notification as a message on the
				topic <code>{"db:<channel>"}</code> from <code>Live.next!</code>
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#topics"}>topics</a>). On Postgres it is <code>pg_notify</code>, so
				another process writing to the database can notify too; on SQLite it stays within the server. It is
				how the job workers sleep until a job is queued.
			</p>

			<Heading id="checked">Checking queries: rocx check --sql</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>rocx check --sql</code> checks an app's SQL with the engine it runs on, and its row types with
				the compiler, without running anything. Each statement written as a string literal at a
				<code>Db.all!</code> or a <code>Db.run!</code> is prepared (never run) on a scratch database, which
				the app's own server builds from its migrations. For SQLite and Turso that is a file it removes
				afterwards; for Postgres, a database it makes on the server <code>DATABASE_URL</code> names and drops
				afterwards. It never migrates a database nobody named for it: without the <code>CREATEDB</code>
				privilege, it says to grant it, or to name a database it may migrate
				(<code>--sql-database URL</code>). <code>rocx dev</code> runs the check after each build.
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"$ rocx check --sql app\napp/Store.roc:29:15: SQLite refuses it: no such column: bodyy in select bodyy as body from notes at offset 7\napp/Store.roc:33:16: parameter $2 is given, and the statement doesn't use it\napp/Store.roc:50:10: `body` is read as an integer, and notes.body is a TEXT (text)\napp/Store.roc:58:10: `note` is read as a plain value, and notes.note may be null (it has no `not null`): read it as Db.Nullable(…)\napp/Store.roc:66:10: `title` isn't a column the statement returns (it returns id)\nrocx check --sql: 7 statements checked on SQLite (the rows of 5 against their records): 5 problems"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The SQL.</strong> The engine judges the statement as it reads it: its syntax, its tables,
					columns and functions, Postgres's types, and what Turso doesn't support yet. The parameters a call
					gives as a list must be exactly the statement's <code>$1</code> to <code>$n</code>, each used.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The rows.</strong> For a <code>Db.all!</code> whose rows are annotated
					(<code>rows : List(T)</code>, or the result of the function the call is in), it compares the record
					with the statement's columns: a field the statement doesn't return, a type the column's values
					can't decode into, and a plain field over a column that may be null (it has no
					<code>not null</code>), which <code>Db.Nullable</code> should read. rocx writes the type into a
					small app of its own, beside a copy of yours, and reads it through the compiler's parser
					(<code>packages/dbcheck</code>), so an alias, a nominal type or a package's type is read as the
					compiler has it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What it can't check:</strong> SQL built from strings
					(<code>{"\"select \${columns} from notes\""}</code>) or passed in a variable, which it counts and lists
					with <code>--verbose</code>; and a statement on a table the app creates while it runs rather than
					in a migration, which you mark with <code>Db.unchecked(sql)</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Where to run it:</strong> by hand, in CI, and in <code>just examples</code> for each
					example. <code>--engine turso</code> asks Turso about a SQLite app (or SQLite about a Turso app).
					Describing a statement takes milliseconds; most of a check is the server starting and the
					migrations.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="turso">Turso: SQLite's SQL on another engine</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Turso(path)</code> is <code>Sqlite(path)</code> with another engine underneath:
				<a href="https://github.com/tursodatabase/turso">Turso</a>, SQLite rewritten in Rust, also inside the
				server's process. Its SQL is SQLite's and its file uses SQLite's format, so everything this page says
				of SQLite holds for it too: the app's SQL, and a package's or a plugin's migrations and needs for
				SQLite. <code>Db.backend!</code> answers <code>Sqlite</code> for both; <code>Db.engine!</code> tells
				them apart.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started| Ok({}),\n\tmiddleware: Middlewares.default,\n\tdatabase: Turso(\"data/plate.db\"),\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				The URL picks the engine: <code>--database-url turso:data/plate.db</code> runs a SQLite app on
				Turso, and <code>sqlite:</code> runs a Turso app on SQLite, which is how you try one against the
				other.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>What Turso adds: writes at the same time.</strong> Its file is in MVCC mode. A transaction
				(<code>Db.transaction!</code>) begins <code>concurrent</code>, on a snapshot of its own, and writes
				beside the others. When two write the same row, the one that commits second rolls back, and its
				body runs again (up to a hundred times). So keep what isn't the database's (a fetch, an email) out
				of a transaction. A write outside a transaction is its own concurrent transaction, retried the same
				way; a schema change (a migration) takes the file to itself. Two transactions that read rows and
				write different ones both commit, where on SQLite the second would wait for the first.
			</p>
			<p>What differs:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					In MVCC mode the file is Turso's alone; SQLite refuses to open it.
					<code>--database-url turso:data/plate.db?mvcc=off</code>, run once, gives it back (and in that mode
					Turso's writes take turns, as SQLite's do).
				</li>
				<li>
					The server keeps a Turso file to itself while it runs (an OS lock), so <code>sqlite3</code>, or a
					test's <code>node:sqlite</code>, opens it only once the server has stopped.
				</li>
				<li>
					Turso has no FTS5, so the full-text search in <code>examples/notes</code> and
					<code>examples/plate</code> needs SQLite. It has no <code>order by</code> inside an aggregate either
					(<code>json_group_array(value order by key)</code>, which <code>examples/board</code> uses).
				</li>
				<li>
					Turso is younger than SQLite (0.8, before its 1.0). Once it has checkpointed rows, deleting many
					of them slows what follows badly until its next checkpoint. Running
					<code>pragma wal_checkpoint(truncate)</code> after such a delete avoids it; <code>?mvcc=off</code>
					doesn't have the problem.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<a href={Paths.examples_sqlite_vs_turso}>examples/sqlite-vs-turso</a> times the same work on both,
				in Roc, in the server (writes, reads, scans, JSON, and eight callers at once), and its page has the
				numbers.
			</p>

			<Heading id="roc-pg">roc-pg: queries checked at build</Heading>
			<p>
				For Postgres, <a href="https://github.com/niclas-ahden/roc-pg">roc-pg</a> is a client written in Roc.
				It speaks Postgres's protocol over a socket the platform gives it (<code>Tcp</code>), signs in using
				the platform's <code>Random</code>, and checks each query against a schema when the app is compiled:
				a column that isn't there, a parameter of the wrong type, or a nullable column read as one that
				can't be null each fails the build. The platform's example (<code>server/examples/roc-pg</code>)
				uses the same <code>schema.sql</code> as its migration, so what roc-pg checks against is what the
				server applied.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_rocpg.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_rocpg.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Use either, or both. <code>Db</code> has a pool, transactions that take jobs with them, and rows
				decoded into the record you annotate, with <code>rocx check --sql</code> to check them. roc-pg has
				queries the compiler checks, and a connection per use (no pool). Checking the example takes under a
				second from cold, and an optimized build about six; the platform's tests build it and run it
				against Postgres.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The check's blind spots:</strong> SQL built from strings or passed in a variable, a row
					whose type isn't annotated where rocx can read it, a column from the outer side of a
					<code>left join</code> (taken as its table says), and, on SQLite, an expression's type and
					nullability. Those still show when the query runs (<code>BadRow</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Parameters</strong> are four kinds (null, text, integer, boolean) and arrays of them: no
					floats (pass a number as <code>Text</code>, which Postgres reads as the type its place wants) and
					no bytes yet. A row reads an array column through a cast (<code>array_to_json(tags)</code>).
				</li>
				<li><strong>No cursors or streaming:</strong> a query's rows come back whole.</li>
				<li><strong>SQLite</strong> is a file: no in-memory database (the server's connections couldn't share it), and a blob is read through <code>hex(…)</code>.</li>
				<li><strong>Turso</strong>'s sync to Turso's cloud and its own full-text search aren't used yet; in MVCC mode it has no <code>WITHOUT ROWID</code> tables and no <code>REINDEX</code>.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
